From bdab3bb7e341c96cc5b1f698fdc56cbfde70c61d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:09:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix braced initializer for scalar variables

C allows the initializer of a scalar to be a single expression enclosed
in braces (C89 3.5.7, C99 6.7.8p11), e.g. `const char *p = { 0 };` or
`int n = { 3 };`.  The parser handed the brace to the expression parser,
which built an EX_AGGREGATE node that nothing could consume: a static or
global variable gave "Non constant initializer" once it was referenced,
a local gave "Unexpected aggregate", and a braced scalar inside a struct
or array initializer gave "Constant initializer expected".

The scalar branches of ParseConstInitExpression and ParseVarInitExpression
now accept an optional pair of braces around the initializer, with an
optional trailing comma.  More than one expression is reported once as
"Too many initializers for scalar" and the rest of the list is skipped;
a missing closing brace is reported as "'}' expected".  Braced and
unbraced declaration initializers generate identical code (checked byte
for byte on the tested forms); C++ braced call arguments and functional
casts go through a temporary, as they did before for aggregates, so their
code can differ from the plain form at low optimization levels.

The same two functions are reached by scalar compound literals and by
C++ braced initialization (default member initializers, member
initializer lists, braced call arguments, functional casts), so those
forms are accepted too.

Two new autotests: scalarbraceinittest.c (global, static, static-local
and local scalars of several types, trailing comma, run-time and
side-effecting initializers, compound literals, braced scalar members
of structs and arrays with and without brace elision) and
scalarbraceinitcpp.cpp (the C++ forms above).

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
